A||||4||brianp1fiero||Hey V-8 Guys||||1||
Z||000000||brianp1fiero||08-12-2000||04:26 PM||||Does any one know how to make the factory tach work?  Is the tach filter needed?  I had a V-6 originally.  Any help is appreciated.<p>[This message has been edited by brianp1fiero (edited 08-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spyder||08-12-2000||07:30 PM||||V8Archie will recalibrate from 4 or 6 cylinder to 8 cylinder for $ 30.00.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||08-12-2000||08:41 PM||||Hello brianp1fiero. To make your tach read properly with the V8 you will need to modify your tachometer. I believe the tach uses a asimple RC (resistor/capacitor) network. You can change either the capacitor or the resistor. If you want to change the resistor, then you could use a multi-turn variable one and dial it in perfectly for maximum precision.<P>Check out Oliver's tach page for more info:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/Tach.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/Tach.html</A> <P>And of course you could just change the capacitor but the value that you use depends on whether you had a 4 or 6 to begin with.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fsrowsell||08-12-2000||11:29 PM||||Been looking for a way to modify a Fiero/GM tachometer to work with a 4, 6 or 8 cylinder engine?<BR>Here's how:<P>There is a capacitor on top of the tachometer circut board, it looks like a silver barrel with<BR>leads coming out of each end. Replace this part with one from the list below:<P><BR>4 Cylinder : 0.01 uF<BR>6 Cylinder : 0.0082 uF<BR>8 Cylinder : 0.0064 uF<P><BR>Thanks to Jelly for this information!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||brianp1fiero||08-13-2000||11:51 AM||||Thanks for the info.  I believe I can change the resistor without any problems.  Hey dman, that link was most helpful.<P>Brian ||||reg||1
